592186045417] [17592186566993 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186566560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566993 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "efe77e00-1a52-5b52-ba41-a1e72bc9367b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566999 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566999 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566999 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566999 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566999 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566999 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566999 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566999 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186566549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566999 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f573bee3-51b2-5b6f-b283-d4ab5524dc98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567016 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567016 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141985" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567016 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567016 #edb/ref 1071 "${x}^{2}y\\cdot {({x}^{3}y)}^{2}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567016 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11621803-c2eb-5773-8ffe-0f4ac7a22e24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567016 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by using the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y\\cdot {({x}^{3}y)}^{2}={x}^{2}y\\cdot ({x}^{3})^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y\\cdot ({x}^{3})^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}y\\cdot {x}^{3\\cdot 2}\\cdot {y}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}y\\cdot {x}^{6}\\cdot {y}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y\\cdot {x}^{6}\\cdot {y}^{2} & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{6})\\cdot (y\\cdot {y}^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2+6}\\cdot {y}^{1+2} & = \\\\ {x}^{8}\\cdot {y}^{3} & = \\\\ {x}^{8}{y}^{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567022 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567022 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567022 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor $100+40y-5{y}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a0b67f9-e70a-56fd-9ed9-04486a79a002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567022 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we can factor out a $(-5)$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100+40y-5{y}^{2} & =-5({y}^{2}-8y-20) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${y}^{2}-8y-20$ in parentheses.\n\nWe start by listing the factor pairs of the constant term $(-20).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-20) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 20 \\\\ 2 & \\times (-10) \\\\ (-2) & \\times 10 \\\\ 4 & \\times (-5) \\\\ (-4) & \\times 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-20) & =-19 & \\times  \\\\ -1+20 & =19 & \\times  \\\\ 2+(-10) & =-8 & ✓ \\\\ -2+10 & =8 & \\times  \\\\ 4+(-5) & =-1 & \\times  \\\\ -4+5 & =1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $-8y=2y-10y$ and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({y}^{2}-8y-20) & = \\\\ -5({y}^{2}+2y-10y-20) & = \\\\ -5(({y}^{2}+2y)+(-10y-20)) & = \\\\ -5(y(y+2)-10(y+2)) & = \\\\ -5(y-10)(y+2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567028 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567028 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567028 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567028 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/06/06616bd59152ad40f051177a319b6a54c4e6df2c60ba04bc6056fa3ceea98c03.png)\n\nConsider $△PQR$ shown above. The line segment $\\overset{―}{QH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PR}.$ If $m∠QRP={155}^{∘},$ calculate $m∠RQH.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567028 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b6ddb3f-4ad3-5b3b-b8b2-23791ddb8dd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567028 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the straight angle must have a measure of ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QRP+m∠HRQ & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {155}^{∘}+m∠HRQ & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HRQ & ={25}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $△QHR$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠RQH+m∠HRQ+m∠QHR & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠RQH+{25}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠RQH+{115}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠RQH & ={65}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567029 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567029 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567029 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{288a}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567029 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2bd15f40-e989-5300-b551-e3144c2c681f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567029 #edb/ref 1074 "We first write $288a$ as the product $144\\cdot 2a$ which contains a perfect square $(144):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{288a} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{144\\cdot 2a} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{144\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(12)^{2}\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(12)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(12)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ |12|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 12\\sqrt{2a} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567039 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567039 #edb/ref 1069 "q-223771" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567039 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567039 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are rational?\n\nI. $0.1222$\n\nII. $1.3\\overset{―}{4}$\n\nIII. $1.981354…$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567039 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "545ece5b-8e3c-584b-b6c7-99fcc1c7f1e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567039 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of our numbers in turn.\n\n- $0.1222$ is a rational number because it can be expressed as a ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n0.1222=\\frac{1\\,222}{10\\,000}\n$$\n\n- $1.3\\overset{―}{4}$ is a rational number. The notation $\\overset{―}{4}$ tells us that the digit $4$ after the decimal point repeats indefinitely.\n\n$$\n1.3\\overset{―}{4}=1.34444444444…\n$$\n\nAny decimal with repeating digits can be represented as a ratio of two integers. In this case,\n\n$$\n1.3\\overset{―}{4}=\\frac{121}{90}.\n$$\n\n- $1.981354…$ is not a rational number. The notation \"$…$\" tells us that digits after the decimal point extend forever. However, there are no repeating digits.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567051 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567051 #edb/ref 1069 "q-28880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567051 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567051 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the inequalities, $x<3$ and $x>-1$, express their common set of solutions as an interval." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567051 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "737200e6-c3ef-51a6-b724-78dcb92c8633" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567051 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by sketching the solutions to the two inequalities separately:\n\n![](images/16/166ce99c0ceedf97d38066b544b5dc6ff7ca63296572d952536193a83a410d94.png)\n\n![](images/b5/b5a0212c7df84c064e5b49f878c47f824631bf9585bf20a7a48ce8ddc1b4a646.png)\n\nThe common set of values consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/25/25313fabbd318c3c1fdc5e63b0d20ac44b5a4c951f70232dbefaaa2d82db4045.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n$(-1,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567084 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567084 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147068" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567084 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567084 #edb/ref 1071 "When a coin was tossed $14$ times, $\\frac{4}{7}$ of the tosses landed heads up. How many times did it come up with tails?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567084 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c76e6329-2e41-5111-9e2f-17e316c25286" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567084 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given that the ratio of heads to the total number of tosses is $\\frac{4}{7}.$ \n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of heads, and the denominator represents the total number of tosses.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $14$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\times 2}{7\\times 2}=\\frac{8}{14}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it came up with heads $8$ times. \nSo, the number of tails is\n\n$$\n14-8=6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567102 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567102 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567102 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567102 #edb/ref 1071 "The time, in hours, it takes to empty a pool varies inversely with the rate of pumping, in gallons per hour ($\\text{gph}$). If it takes $5$ hours to empty the pool at a rate of $200\\,\\text{gph},$ what is the constant of variation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567102 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef0090d7-e552-5522-8456-ce718437e171" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567102 #edb/ref 1074 "The time $t$ is inversely proportional to the rate of pumping $r,$ so we have\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{k}{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that it takes $5$ hours to empty the pool at a rate of $200\\,\\text{gph}.$  We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =\\frac{k}{200} \\\\ 5\\cdot 200 & =k \\\\ 1\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=1\\,000.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567106 #edb/ref 1069 "q-223197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567106 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567106 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c5/c543b90535e3b252954ffb9e935f3f05a71d302f1f69a5212b467c826c9d8697.png)\n\nGiven the quadrilateral above, we have $x=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567106 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3b5ac18-7c76-53e4-b7d2-3ac62a11ea93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567106 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+(6x+{30}^{∘})+(5x+{30}^{∘})+4x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 20x+{60}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 20x & ={300}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567115 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186567149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567115 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10a1f6ef-362d-5631-86f1-b71f57ec2d11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567115 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567117 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23c11523-25f4-517f-9af8-e0e0997771bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567117 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186567027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567119 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a7599e0-d833-5e8d-955f-489466bc1e3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567119 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186567084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567120 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186567130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567120 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "36eb5e9e-96be-54e1-89dd-a085dd36d242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567120 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186567039 #